<!--
  Shipped inside the package as build/<PackageId>.targets, which NuGet imports into any project that
  references this package directly.

  Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi carries an XML comment source generator whose output uses C#
  interceptors, and enables the namespace for that output through build assets of its own. NuGet does
  not flow build assets transitively, so a consumer who takes this companion — and nothing else,
  which is exactly what docs/openapi.en.md tells them to do — gets the generator without the property
  that makes its output legal, and the build fails on CS9137 in generated code they never wrote.

  A .targets and not a .props, deliberately. NuGet imports build/*.props above the project body and
  build/*.targets below it, so a consumer who assigns InterceptorsNamespaces rather than appending to
  it would silently overwrite a .props and get CS9137 back. Microsoft ships this same property from a
  .targets for that reason, and their package survives that assignment; a .props version of this file
  was measured not to.

  It enables exactly the namespace Microsoft enables, and nothing else.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <InterceptorsNamespaces>$(InterceptorsNamespaces);Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi.Generated</InterceptorsNamespaces>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
